    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The most widely discussed biological theory in this context is the \"Brain Drain\" hypothesis, which suggests that high fertility rates in some ancestral populations were supported by lower cognitive processing speeds to conserve energy for reproduction. Conversely, the \"Cool Brain\" hypothesis argues that ancestors facing higher environmental risks developed slower processing speeds to enhance planning and risk mitigation. Finally, the \"Brain Gain\" hypothesis posits that specific temperature changes like climate cooling favored faster metabolism and higher cold tolerance in humans to enable efficient social facilitation.",
